WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.090
Welcome to Module 13 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:06.862
run the managed service account standard.

3
00:00:06.862 --> 00:00:10.760
It teaches the standard in the
companion guide Service Accounts,

4
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.287
with Chapter 12 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:13.287 --> 00:00:15.937
Module 12 moved one account by hand.

6
00:00:15.937 --> 00:00:21.821
This module is about every account after
that: how a G M S A is asked for, approved,

7
00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:26.417
created, watched, attested and
retired the same way each time,

8
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:28.163
with the same evidence.

9
00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:31.120
Take it after Modules 8 and 12.

10
00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:35.882
Why a standard?

11
00:00:35.882 --> 00:00:39.977
Module 12 moved one account,
with Microsoft Learn's commands,

12
00:00:39.977 --> 00:00:42.270
and the kit planned the same moves.

13
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:47.492
The standard makes every managed service
account after that go the same way:

14
00:00:47.492 --> 00:00:49.978
asked for, approved, created, watched,

15
00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:53.708
attested and retired, with
the same evidence each time.

16
00:00:53.708 --> 00:00:55.757
It splits the work three ways.

17
00:00:55.757 --> 00:01:00.608
SailPoint approves, reached through the
agency's system access management tool.

18
00:01:00.608 --> 00:01:03.833
ServiceNow records the
change and the findings.

19
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:06.666
And Microsoft's tools create and enforce:

20
00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:12.002
Active Directory PowerShell through
Chapter 6's approved plan, Group Policy,

21
00:01:12.002 --> 00:01:17.020
Defender for Identity and, when the
workload moves, Microsoft Entra.

22
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.610
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

23
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.257
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

24
00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:30.581
You'll name who does what in the
standard: the access management tool,

25
00:01:30.581 --> 00:01:33.669
SailPoint, ServiceNow,
the pipeline and a person.

26
00:01:33.669 --> 00:01:38.311
You'll give the standard's settings:
the name, the O U, the retriever group,

27
00:01:38.311 --> 00:01:42.311
encryption, the password interval,
outbound-only, the owners,

28
00:01:42.311 --> 00:01:45.832
the certification cadence
and the ninety-day window.

29
00:01:45.832 --> 00:01:50.796
You'll explain why machines run every step
they can, and where people still must.

30
00:01:50.796 --> 00:01:55.808
And you'll run Chapter 12's checks on
fixtures, and read the findings, the Chapter

31
00:01:55.808 --> 00:02:00.820
6 inputs, the certifications, the ServiceNow
findings and the retirement list.

32
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.057
Before you start, finish Modules 8 and 12.

33
00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:10.000
You need PowerShell 7.1 or later with
Pester 5, and no tenant or domain.

34
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.562
Unit 1 is who does what.

35
00:02:15.562 --> 00:02:20.902
The agency's system access management tool
is the request front end: an owner asks

36
00:02:20.902 --> 00:02:26.001
for, changes or retires an account there,
and it hands the request to SailPoint.

37
00:02:26.001 --> 00:02:29.150
It never approves or
writes anything itself.

38
00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:34.767
SailPoint approves every request, whatever
its risk tier, aggregates the accounts,

39
00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:39.000
correlates each one to its owners
and runs the certifications.

40
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.950
It never writes a G M S
A in Active Directory.

41
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.852
ServiceNow holds the change
record, the typed findings and

42
00:02:46.852 --> 00:02:50.675
the decommission ticket, and
closes a task only on a re-read.

43
00:02:50.675 --> 00:02:52.487
It never approves the account.

44
00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:57.957
The pipeline runs Chapter 6's approved
plan as G M S A dash M S A pipe,

45
00:02:57.957 --> 00:03:01.363
with only the delegated rights it needs.

46
00:03:01.363 --> 00:03:07.088
And a person still runs the D M S A
steps, the uninstall and every delete.

47
00:03:07.088 --> 00:03:10.421
The pipeline's identity
is itself a G M S A,

48
00:03:10.421 --> 00:03:15.130
with two owners, a sponsor
and its own certification.

49
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.330
The standard's settings.

50
00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:26.092
The name is G M S A dash app, or D M S
A dash app, fifteen characters or fewer,

51
00:03:26.092 --> 00:03:30.742
and Microsoft Learn says it
must be unique in the forest.

52
00:03:30.742 --> 00:03:35.134
It lives in a dedicated service-account
O U, where only the creators

53
00:03:35.134 --> 00:03:39.367
group may create one, and that
group's only member is the pipeline.

54
00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:43.930
Its password retrievers are always
one domain local group of the hosts.

55
00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:49.418
Encryption is A E S two fifty-six only,
and the password interval is thirty days,

56
00:03:49.418 --> 00:03:53.705
stated at creation, because Learn
says it can be set only then.

57
00:03:53.705 --> 00:03:59.517
A G M S A with no S P N is outbound-only,
unless there's a recorded reason.

58
00:03:59.517 --> 00:04:02.555
Every account has two named
technical owners and a

59
00:04:02.555 --> 00:04:05.593
federal-employee sponsor,
and no privileged group.

60
00:04:05.593 --> 00:04:09.785
The two owners certify it in
SailPoint: quarterly for R one,

61
00:04:09.785 --> 00:04:12.755
twice a year for R two,
annually for R three.

62
00:04:12.755 --> 00:04:15.399
And it stays disabled for ninety days,

63
00:04:15.399 --> 00:04:18.910
with no sign-in seen,
before anyone deletes it.

64
00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:27.673
Unit 2 is the standard's callout:
machines run the steps, people decide.

65
00:04:27.673 --> 00:04:32.775
Every step a machine or a script can run,
it runs: the request's checks, the plan,

66
00:04:32.775 --> 00:04:37.054
the Active Directory writes, the
re-read that closes the ticket,

67
00:04:37.054 --> 00:04:41.334
the monitoring, the certification
campaigns and the evidence.

68
00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:44.457
People decide, approve in
SailPoint, accept risk,

69
00:04:44.457 --> 00:04:48.985
and run the few steps that need Domain
Admins or that delete something.

70
00:04:48.985 --> 00:04:51.829
This lines up with the
federal requirements better

71
00:04:51.829 --> 00:04:54.673
than the same work done by
hand in a console or portal.

72
00:04:54.673 --> 00:05:02.098
NIST SP 800-53 has enhancements for
automated account management, disabling

73
00:05:02.098 --> 00:05:06.997
accounts and automated audit actions: A
C two, enhancements one, three and four.

74
00:05:06.997 --> 00:05:12.873
FedRAMP's K S I dash I A M dash A A
M is Automating Account Management.

75
00:05:12.873 --> 00:05:16.391
And CISA's Zero Trust Maturity
Model makes automation

76
00:05:16.391 --> 00:05:19.373
and orchestration a
cross-cutting capability.

77
00:05:19.373 --> 00:05:22.473
Automation has limits, and
the standard keeps them.

78
00:05:22.473 --> 00:05:24.901
Separation of duties keeps the approver,

79
00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:29.043
the pipeline's owner and the person
who deletes apart, the authorizing

80
00:05:29.043 --> 00:05:33.472
official accepts the pipeline, and the
pipeline's own identity is governed.

81
00:05:33.472 --> 00:05:37.450
Automation supports a requirement;
the assessor decides.

82
00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.862
Unit 3 follows one G M
S A through the steps.

83
00:05:44.862 --> 00:05:49.079
It's requested, and once SailPoint
approves it, Chapter 12 checks every

84
00:05:49.079 --> 00:05:53.125
field of the approved request before
anything is created: that's S1.

85
00:05:53.125 --> 00:05:57.550
It's decided, with the guide's decision
path giving the target, in S2.

86
00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:02.138
SailPoint approves it, and ServiceNow
opens the change record, in S3.

87
00:06:02.138 --> 00:06:06.432
The pipeline creates it from
the approved plan, in the O U,

88
00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:10.913
A E S two fifty-six, thirty days
and its retriever group, in S4.

89
00:06:10.913 --> 00:06:15.125
It's installed on the hosts and
restricted with Group Policy, in S5.

90
00:06:15.125 --> 00:06:16.984
SailPoint aggregates it,

91
00:06:16.984 --> 00:06:21.588
and the ServiceNow task closes only
on Chapter 12's re-read, in S6.

92
00:06:21.588 --> 00:06:27.400
It's watched with Defender for Identity,
in S7, and certified by its owners, in S8.

93
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:30.262
It's handed over when
an owner leaves, in S9.

94
00:06:30.262 --> 00:06:34.294
And it's retired, in S10: the
pipeline disables it and moves it,

95
00:06:34.294 --> 00:06:39.149
and after ninety days with no sign-in,
a person uninstalls and deletes it.

96
00:06:39.149 --> 00:06:42.870
S11 is the move to Entra,
when the workload moves.

97
00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.345
Try it.

98
00:06:47.345 --> 00:06:51.745
Lab 1 runs in the non-person
identity folder, with no domain.

99
00:06:51.745 --> 00:06:53.971
These are Chapter 12's Lab 1 steps,

100
00:06:53.971 --> 00:06:57.270
and the chapter shows each
one's expected output.

101
00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:02.660
Step one runs the standard's checks on
the fixtures, as of November 20, 2026:

102
00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:08.590
five requests, nine Active Directory
managed accounts and eight register rows,

103
00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:11.195
each built to trip one or more rules.

104
00:07:11.195 --> 00:07:15.195
Look for the requests line:
two passed, and three are held.

105
00:07:15.195 --> 00:07:18.051
Then the Chapter 6 inputs,
the certifications,

106
00:07:18.051 --> 00:07:21.283
and the retirement list,
which ends nothing deleted.

107
00:07:21.283 --> 00:07:24.494
Then one line per rule,
and the manifest's hash.

108
00:07:24.494 --> 00:07:27.871
The script writes its output
folder and nothing else:

109
00:07:27.871 --> 00:07:33.140
nothing to Active Directory, SailPoint,
ServiceNow or Defender for Identity.

110
00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.015
Step two reads the
findings, sorted by rule.

111
00:07:40.015 --> 00:07:42.461
Rules oh one to oh nine
check the requests,

112
00:07:42.461 --> 00:07:46.059
ten to nineteen reconcile
SailPoint with Active Directory,

113
00:07:46.059 --> 00:07:50.879
twenty to twenty-three are the certifications,
twenty-four to twenty-six compare

114
00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:55.628
Defender for Identity's list, and twenty-seven
to twenty-nine are the retirement.

115
00:07:55.628 --> 00:07:59.428
Find the three findings
on G M S A dash ops.

116
00:07:59.428 --> 00:08:03.739
N P I S T D ten: SailPoint
never aggregated it.

117
00:08:03.739 --> 00:08:06.227
Sixteen: it's in Domain Admins.

118
00:08:06.227 --> 00:08:08.627
Eighteen: no request explains it.

119
00:08:08.627 --> 00:08:11.578
That's the drift rule,
and it runs every time.

120
00:08:11.578 --> 00:08:14.306
Defender for Identity's rules show up too:

121
00:08:14.306 --> 00:08:18.800
S V C dash backup is a user account
still serving as a service account,

122
00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.176
M S A dash print oh one is a standalone
managed service account, and rule twenty-six

123
00:08:24.176 --> 00:08:29.510
lists three accounts Defender for
Identity sees that the register doesn't.

124
00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:36.347
Step three reads what the
code hands to Chapter 6.

125
00:08:36.347 --> 00:08:41.701
The two requests that passed become
two rows of the replatform file:

126
00:08:41.701 --> 00:08:45.811
a D M S A for the billing account
and a G M S A for payroll.

127
00:08:45.811 --> 00:08:49.307
Check that both rows carry a
request I D and a change record:

128
00:08:49.307 --> 00:08:53.460
the SailPoint request and the
ServiceNow change that approved them.

129
00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.184
The retrievers file has one entry,

130
00:08:56.184 --> 00:09:00.173
for the payroll G M S A,
naming its one group of hosts.

131
00:09:00.173 --> 00:09:06.885
The D M S A has no entry, because a D M S
A learns its hosts during its migration.

132
00:09:06.885 --> 00:09:12.190
These files go into Chapter 6's reviewed
inputs in a reviewed pull request,

133
00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:17.060
and Chapter 6's plan refuses a write
with no request or change record.

134
00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:20.090
So the plan creates
exactly what was approved.

135
00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.865
Step four reads the certifications
and the ServiceNow findings.

136
00:09:27.865 --> 00:09:30.826
Each account is due by its risk tier:

137
00:09:30.826 --> 00:09:35.828
R one every three months, R two
every six, R three every twelve.

138
00:09:35.828 --> 00:09:42.752
G M S A dash print is R three, last
certified in October 2025, so it's overdue.

139
00:09:42.752 --> 00:09:46.090
And its owner revoked it, so
it becomes a stale access

140
00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:49.427
finding for ServiceNow,
without anyone retyping it.

141
00:09:49.427 --> 00:09:54.352
The second finding is an orphan
account, on G M S A dash web.

142
00:09:54.352 --> 00:09:57.406
Look back at step two's
warning on the same account:

143
00:09:57.406 --> 00:10:01.502
a certification decided by someone
who isn't an owner doesn't count.

144
00:10:01.502 --> 00:10:05.500
The code writes these findings as
the payload a ServiceNow import

145
00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:09.710
or the integration kit's client
posts; it doesn't post them itself.

146
00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.723
Step five reads the retirement list.

147
00:10:15.723 --> 00:10:19.826
Only one account is a delete
candidate: G M S A dash legacy,

148
00:10:19.826 --> 00:10:24.197
disabled for a hundred and eleven
days with no sign-in since.

149
00:10:24.197 --> 00:10:28.785
G M S A dash batch signed in after
it was disabled, so it isn't one.

150
00:10:28.785 --> 00:10:32.525
G M S A dash report is still
enabled in Active Directory,

151
00:10:32.525 --> 00:10:35.185
though the register says it's disabled.

152
00:10:35.185 --> 00:10:39.666
And G M S A dash oldfax has been
disabled for only fifty days,

153
00:10:39.666 --> 00:10:41.347
so it waits for ninety.

154
00:10:41.347 --> 00:10:45.561
Who deletes G M S A dash
legacy, and through what?

155
00:10:45.561 --> 00:10:47.973
A person, through the decommission ticket.

156
00:10:47.973 --> 00:10:52.722
The code never deletes: it says which
accounts a person may delete, and why.

157
00:10:52.722 --> 00:10:56.210
Step six cleans up by
removing the output folder.

158
00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:04.546
Lab 2 runs the same checks on a test
domain's exports from SailPoint

159
00:11:04.546 --> 00:11:08.298
and Defender for Identity, with
a live read of the G M S A's.

160
00:11:08.298 --> 00:11:09.859
The authors haven't run it.

161
00:11:09.859 --> 00:11:15.933
Use a test Active Directory domain, never
production, after Chapter 6's Lab 2:

162
00:11:15.933 --> 00:11:18.871
a K D S root key, the service-account O U,

163
00:11:18.871 --> 00:11:22.985
the delegated creators group
and at least one G M S A.

164
00:11:22.985 --> 00:11:27.104
You also need a SailPoint sandbox
with an Active Directory source

165
00:11:27.104 --> 00:11:30.923
scoped to that O U, and Defender
for Identity on the test domain.

166
00:11:30.923 --> 00:11:34.878
Write the test domain's standard
settings, then put the exports

167
00:11:34.878 --> 00:11:38.834
in the kit's import formats,
mapping each product column by hand.

168
00:11:38.834 --> 00:11:42.291
Run the checks with a
domain controller named,

169
00:11:42.291 --> 00:11:44.747
so the script reads the G M S A's live.

170
00:11:44.747 --> 00:11:48.194
A G M S A you created by
hand in Chapter 6's Lab 2,

171
00:11:48.194 --> 00:11:54.322
with no request in the register, is rule
eighteen: that's the drift rule working.

172
00:11:54.322 --> 00:11:57.830
The manifest shows one
read, and nothing else.

173
00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:02.967
The federal requirement.

174
00:12:02.967 --> 00:12:05.831
Each step of the standard
names what it supports,

175
00:12:05.831 --> 00:12:09.481
and these are the rows this
module's lab produces evidence for.

176
00:12:09.481 --> 00:12:13.989
The request check in S1 supports A C
two, Account Management, and I A four,

177
00:12:13.989 --> 00:12:19.120
Identifier Management, in NIST SP 800-53,

178
00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:23.318
with the SailPoint approval and
the ServiceNow change beside it.

179
00:12:23.318 --> 00:12:27.011
The reconciliation in
S6 supports C M eight,

180
00:12:27.011 --> 00:12:31.730
System Component Inventory,
and A C six, Least Privilege.

181
00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:37.772
The certifications and the ServiceNow
findings in S8 support A C two and A C six,

182
00:12:37.772 --> 00:12:41.305
enhancement seven, Review
of User Privileges.

183
00:12:41.305 --> 00:12:45.593
And the retirement list in S10,
with the decommission ticket,

184
00:12:45.593 --> 00:12:49.367
supports A C two, enhancement
three, Disable Accounts.

185
00:12:49.367 --> 00:12:54.005
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

186
00:12:54.005 --> 00:12:58.300
A row provides evidence for a
control; the assessor decides.

187
00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:04.025
Check yourself with two questions.

188
00:13:04.025 --> 00:13:08.925
First: who creates a G M S A in
production under the standard?

189
00:13:08.925 --> 00:13:13.031
The answer is b: the pipeline,
as G M S A dash M S A pipe,

190
00:13:13.031 --> 00:13:18.575
running Chapter 6's approved plan
with only the delegated rights.

191
00:13:18.575 --> 00:13:21.005
SailPoint never writes a G M S A,

192
00:13:21.005 --> 00:13:25.088
and ServiceNow's Active
Directory spoke isn't used.

193
00:13:25.088 --> 00:13:29.912
Second: an R two G M S A was
last certified seven months ago.

194
00:13:29.912 --> 00:13:31.701
What does Chapter 12 report?

195
00:13:31.701 --> 00:13:36.217
The answer is b: N P I S T D
twenty, certification overdue,

196
00:13:36.217 --> 00:13:39.913
because R two is certified
every six months.

197
00:13:39.913 --> 00:13:42.340
The module page has three more questions,

198
00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.190
each linked to the
section that answers it.

199
00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:49.765
To sum up.

200
00:13:49.765 --> 00:13:53.502
The standard makes every managed
service account the same:

201
00:13:53.502 --> 00:13:58.460
asked for in the agency's system access
management tool, approved in SailPoint,

202
00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:03.418
recorded in ServiceNow, created by the
pipeline, restricted with Group Policy,

203
00:14:03.418 --> 00:14:07.841
watched with Defender for Identity,
certified by its two owners on its

204
00:14:07.841 --> 00:14:12.265
risk tier's cadence, and retired after
ninety days disabled, by a person.

205
00:14:12.265 --> 00:14:17.002
Chapter 12 checks each of those steps
on every run and writes the evidence.

206
00:14:17.002 --> 00:14:22.094
In this module you ran its checks, found
the drift on G M S A dash ops, read the

207
00:14:22.094 --> 00:14:27.778
inputs it hands to Chapter 6, and read the
certifications and the retirement list.

208
00:14:27.778 --> 00:14:31.428
Chapter 12 goes on with its
evidence file and its tests.

209
00:14:31.428 --> 00:14:34.320
Next, go back to the
learning path's index.
